// @run
// @log 2
// @log 5
// @hint redundant type annotation: `dist` is inferred as `f64`
// The sharper face of the same gap, and the one a real program hits: `dist` is
// an ORDINARY named function, but the module also uses a function value, and a
// module that does routes every "capturing" function through its closure env.
// `sqrt` used to sit in `dist`'s capture list because capture analysis did not
// know the name is a lowering target, so the env build had nothing to read and
// the whole program refused — WITHOUT the lambda on line 12 the same file
// compiled and printed 5.
//
// That is what made this worth closing rather than documenting: it is not a
// lambda restriction, it is "this program contains a lambda ANYWHERE", so
// `std:fmt`'s f64 arm (which cannot avoid `f64bits`) could not be used beside a
// comparator or beside `std:test`.
function dist(x: f64, y: f64): f64 { sqrt(x * x + y * y) }

const inc = (a: i32) => a + 1
print(inc(1))
print(dist(3.0, 4.0))
